+# What a workout is
+
+> **DRAFT for the user to edit.** Written by the agent from TRAINER.md, the glossary and the sources below; nothing here is the user's until they have edited it. Lines marked **Decide** are the questions the draft could not answer on the user's behalf.
+
+The planner reads this as method, not as rules. The engine still checks only legality (ADR-0001); everything below is judgement the agent exercises and explains in each block's `reason`.
+
+## 1. A workout is built from working sets
+
+A **working set** is one round of one exercise taken close to failure. Anything else is warm-up or filler and is not what a workout is made of.
+
+| | default | source |
+|---|---|---|
+| effort | stop 1-3 reps short of failure | [3], [6] |
+| reps | 6-15; up to 20-30 only on band or body-weight work that cannot be loaded further | [4], [5] |
+| too easy | more than ~20 clean reps means the **variant** is wrong: move along `regression_of` / `variant_of` (`compare_variants`) to a harder one, never add a second variant beside it | — |
+| too hard | fewer than 5 reps on body weight: the regression | — |
+| rounds | 3-4 for a working exercise; **1 round is a warm-up**, never a working exercise | [1], [2] |
+
+**Decide:** the reps-in-reserve target, and whether the plan should carry it. The schema has no field for it today (`PlanItem` holds reps or seconds only).
+
+## 2. How many exercises a group gets
+
+Volume is what the time buys, so the count follows from the minutes, not from a menu.
+
+- One working exercise at 3-4 rounds costs **6-10 minutes** at the rest in § 3 (`rounds × (reps × 3 s + rest)`, plus setup).
+- So a group gets roughly **its allocated minutes ÷ 8** exercises, at least one, at most three.
+- Two exercises for one group must differ in **pattern or angle** (a horizontal press and a fly; a curl and a hammer curl). Two variants of the same movement are one exercise prescribed twice.
+- Covering every group once comes before a second exercise for any of them (already in the brief).
+
+| group's minutes | exercises |
+|---|---|
+| under 8 | 1 |
+| 8-18 | 2 |
+| over 18 | 2-3 |
+
+**Decide:** a volume target per muscle per cycle. The literature speaks in sets per **week** (≥10 per muscle [1]); REPS counts rounds per **cycle** (`volume_denominator`), so the number has to be the user's.
+
+## 3. Rest follows intent
+
+Rest is prescribed per block (ADR-0002). Inside a block there is **no rest between exercises**: the rest comes after the whole round.
+
+| intent | example | rest after the round | source |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| strength | compound, ≤ 6 reps | 2.5-3 min, more if needed | [2], [7] |
+| hypertrophy, compound | push-up, row, squat at 6-15 | 90-180 s | [2], [8] |
+| isolation | curl, extension, raise | 60-90 s | [7] |
+| superset of non-competing muscles | curl + triceps extension | 60-120 s after the pair | [9] |
+| conditioning / core circuit | plank, carry, mountain climber | 30-60 s | — |
+
+**Decide:** the hypertrophy band. TRAINER.md says 90-120 s; [2] found 3 min beat 1 min on compounds in trained men. Widening it is a TRAINER.md edit as well.
+
+## 4. Which structure, and when
+
+The block is chosen for what it does to the training, never for what it does to the clock. A circuit is cheap because it pays one rest per round; that saving is only real when the exercises in it do not tire the same muscle.
+
+| structure | reach for it when | never |
+|---|---|---|
+| **straight sets** | the first, heaviest exercise of the workout; anything loaded or high in `required_stability`; the default | — |
+| **superset** | two exercises whose **primary muscles do not overlap**: antagonists (biceps / triceps, push / pull) or upper / lower. It saves real time [9], [10] | two exercises for the same muscle: the second set is done with no rest and the pair is one exercise done badly |
+| **circuit** | warm-up, core, conditioning; or a very short workout (≤ 20 min) that must still touch every group, with each station on a different muscle | variants of one movement; the main work of a group; a way to fit more exercises into the time |
+
+Tests a block should pass, each one written into its `reason`:
+- **Why this structure?** "Antagonists, so the rest overlaps" is a reason. "It fits" is not.
+- **Does any muscle appear twice in it as primary?** Then it is not a superset or a circuit; split it.
+
+## 5. Order
+
+1. Compound before isolation; larger muscles before smaller [7].
+2. The exercise that needs the most skill or stability first, while fresh.
+3. Core and conditioning last, unless they are the workout.
+
+**Decide:** a warm-up. ADR-0002 forbids ramp-up sets on the same exercise, so a warm-up is either a different light exercise in its own block (and costs minutes) or done outside the plan.
+
+## 6. The test case
+
+Chest and arms, 45 minutes, body weight and bands. What the bot produced in session 7:
+
+| | blocks | why it is wrong |
+|---|---|---|
+| block 1 | four push-up variants, 3 rounds, 8 reps | one movement four times, far from failure at 8 reps, no rest between them |
+| block 2 | five triceps exercises, 3 rounds, 10 reps | one muscle five times in a row |
+| total | 25 of 45 min | the clock bought nothing |
+
+What this document asks for instead (exercise names illustrative, ids from `admissible_set`):
+
+| block | structure | rounds × reps | rest | min |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| hardest push-up variant reaching 8-12 | straight sets | 4 × 10 | 120 s | 10 |
+| band fly or decline push-up | straight sets | 3 × 12 | 90 s | 6.3 |
+| band curl + band triceps pushdown | superset | 3 × 12 + 12 | 90 s | 8.1 |
+| bench dips | straight sets | 3 × 10 | 90 s | 6 |
+| band hammer curl + overhead band extension | superset | 3 × 12 + 10 | 60 s | 6.3 |
+| | | | | **37 + setup** |
+
+Chest 7 working rounds, triceps 9, biceps 6, every block with a reason a coach would write.
+
